+        <div class="journal-article" id="journal-article">
+          <p>Every bag of coffee we roast begins long before it reaches our shop, on steep hillsides where the air is
+            thin and the mornings are cool. People often ask us where our beans come from, and the honest answer is
+            that it changes with the seasons. Coffee is an agricultural product, and like any fruit, it has a harvest
+            window. We follow those harvests around the globe, buying from Central America in the spring, East Africa
+            in the summer, and Indonesia and South America as the year winds down. That rhythm is why our menu shifts
+            and why a coffee you loved in March may be replaced by something entirely different by October.</p>
+
+          <h3>Relationships before transactions</h3>
+          <p>We buy the majority of our green coffee through direct relationships with producers and small
+            cooperatives, many of whom we have worked with for more than six years. Our sourcing team visits origin at
+            least twice a year: once during harvest to walk the farms and see the cherries being picked, and once
+            during the off-season to talk about plans, challenges, and investments for the year ahead. These visits
+            are not a formality. They are where we learn that a farm is experimenting with a new drying bed, that a
+            cooperative needs help financing a wet mill, or that a dry spell has thinned out a crop and prices need to
+            reflect it.</p>
+          <p>We commit to purchase volumes before the harvest begins, which gives farmers the security to invest in
+            quality rather than rushing cherries to the cheapest buyer. In exchange, producers set aside their best
+            lots for us to taste first.</p>
+
+          <h3>Paying for quality, transparently</h3>
+          <p>We pay well above the commodity market price, and we publish what we pay. On average, our farm-gate
+            prices last year were more than double the Fairtrade minimum, with premiums tied to cup quality. When a
+            lot scores higher on the cupping table, the producer earns more, full stop. We believe that price
+            transparency is the single most effective tool for making sure the people doing the hardest work in the
+            supply chain are paid fairly, and we share a breakdown of costs from farm to cup on every retail bag.</p>
+
+          <h3>Tasting, tasting, and more tasting</h3>
+          <p>Before we commit to any lot, we cup samples at three stages: a pre-shipment sample at origin, an arrival
+            sample when the container lands, and a final sample once the beans have rested in our warehouse. We are
+            looking for clarity, sweetness, and a sense of place. A washed Ethiopian should taste of jasmine and
+            stone fruit; a natural Brazilian should carry chocolate and roasted nuts. If a coffee loses its character
+            somewhere along the way, we work with the producer to understand why rather than simply walking away.</p>
+
+          <h3>Sustainability at origin</h3>
+          <p>Climate change is reshaping where coffee can grow. Rising temperatures are pushing suitable land higher up
+            the mountains, and unpredictable rainfall is making harvests harder to plan. We set aside a portion of
+            every pound we sell for a producer fund that supports shade-tree planting, water-efficient processing,
+            and soil health programs. Several of our partner farms are now growing climate-resilient varieties
+            alongside their traditional plants, and we have committed to buying those experimental lots even while
+            the farmers are still learning how to get the best from them.</p>
+
+          <h3>From the hopper to your cup</h3>
+          <p>Once the green coffee arrives, we roast in small batches of no more than twelve kilos, adjusting each
+            profile to highlight what makes that particular lot special. Lighter roasts preserve the bright,
+            fruit-forward notes of high-altitude coffees, while slightly longer roasts coax out the body and
+            sweetness of lower-grown beans. Everything we roast is sold within two weeks, so the coffee in your cup
+            is as close as possible to what the farmer tasted at harvest. That is the whole point of the journey: to
+            honor the work that began on a hillside thousands of miles away.</p>
